TODO
====

The design documents in doc/ are the source of truth; start with
doc/README.md. Items are roughly in the order they should be done.

- coil-inductance:
  Compute the inductance of a coil from its geometry (single layer coil
  first: diameter, wire, length, turns), and from the same description its
  series resistance (so Q) and its self-capacitance (a stray capacitance and
  a self-resonant frequency). Clean-room design: Coil64 is GPLv3 and its
  source is not read; the formulas come from the published literature and the
  test vectors from the papers or hand calculations. Rules, sources and the
  plan are in doc/coil.md. Comes after knob-curve; it feeds q-bandwidth and
  stray-capacitance.

- q-bandwidth:
  Quality factor and bandwidth of the tank: BW = f0 / Q, the selectivity
  curve |H(f)|, loaded Q from the source and load resistance, insertion
  loss. Q is entered at first (default 100) and later computed from the coil
  (coil-inductance). Shares the chart code of knob-curve. Not designed yet;
  write doc/q-bandwidth.md first.

- knob-curve:
  Plot frequency against knob position for a tank with range parts, with
  linearity error, sensitivity in Hz per percent, crowding factor, fine tune
  factor against the bare part and a verdict. This is the feature that
  answers whether a padder, trimmer or parallel capacitor leaves a usable
  dial. Design, definitions, interfaces and test vectors are in
  doc/knob-curve.md.

  Phase 1 is src/knob.js (KnobCurve, CurveMetrics, CurveSVG, straight line
  taper only) and the Knob section in the Advanced view. The test vectors
  come from a hand calculation of a 5 to 60 pF capacitor alone, in series
  with 10 pF and in parallel with 100 pF.

- measured-tapers:
  Support tapers other than the straight line capacitance law: straight line
  frequency, straight line wavelength, logarithmic, and tables with
  monotone cubic interpolation. The Mitsumi PVC-2LXT-L5 curves A, B and C
  (doc/polyvaricon.md) are not straight lines (the antenna section is at
  26 percent of its swing at half rotation) and ship as presets. Needs the
  at-sign syntax name@taper in the part grammar, a taper chooser in the part
  panel and user tables stored in config.advanced. Phase 2 of
  doc/knob-curve.md.

- solve-at-knob-position:
  The Solve panel for a selected part holds the other range parts at their
  maximum. It should hold them at their value at a knob position, by default
  the stop that gives the target, and say which position it used. Phase 4 of
  doc/knob-curve.md; needs the taper machinery of measured-tapers.

- tracking-plot:
  Antenna and oscillator tank on one knob, the tracking error
  f_osc - f_rf - IF plotted in kHz with the tracking points marked, the
  image frequency, and a three point tracking solver for the oscillator
  coil, padder and trimmer. This is what the unequal sections of the gang
  capacitors are for. doc/tracking.md; depends on knob-curve and on
  stray-capacitance, because the trimmer and the strays set the minimum
  capacitance the tracking depends on.

- synthesis-padder-trimmer:
  Given a variable part, its range and a target band, compute the fixed
  parallel and series capacitor (or inductor) that make the range cover the
  band, with closed forms and a numeric fall back, then show the resulting
  band and the knob curve metrics. The closed forms are in doc/design-tool.md
  section 2 and have been checked numerically; each still needs a test
  against ThomsonEquation#evaluate.

- e-series-snapping:
  Snap synthesized values to the E12, E24, E48 or E96 series, try the
  neighbour combinations, show the band error at both ends in kHz, and
  offer two standard parts in series or parallel for a value that is not in
  the series. doc/design-tool.md section 2.

- tolerance-analysis:
  Worst case band from the part tolerances. Every resonance falls
  monotonically as any part grows, so the worst case is exact at the corners
  and needs no Monte Carlo (doc/model.md, Monotonicity). Report the edge
  shift in kHz, a ranking of the parts, and optionally an envelope on the
  knob curve. Datasheet tolerances: Mitsumi AM (1 pF + 1 percent), FM
  (0.3 pF + 2 percent). doc/design-tool.md section 3.

- stray-capacitance:
  A stray capacitance in parallel with the C network, entered or measured
  with the LC meter, drawn as a dotted capacitor and included in the result
  table. A 3.8 pF minimum capacitance or a series capacitor of a few pF is
  decided by it. doc/design-tool.md section 4. ComponentNode needs a stray
  flag for the schematic and the tables.

- practicality-checks:
  The warning list under the metrics. Two groups in doc/design-tool.md
  section 5. Structural checks (empty or inverted range, absurd value,
  dominated by one part, parallel inductors, only one of L and C, a range
  with no effect) need only the existing model: build these first, as a
  pure Checks(tank) function and a list under the result table. Build checks
  (value too small, swamped by strays, band lost to tolerance, crowded dial,
  narrow band, trimmer travel, voltage) come with knob-curve,
  stray-capacitance and tolerance-analysis; self-resonance, bandwidth and Q
  checks with coil-inductance and q-bandwidth. Thresholds in one place in the
  code, listed in the document when they are set. Loss, temperature
  coefficient and coil coupling are not modelled and the document says so.

- result-table-part-rows:
  The result table of the Advanced mode is built from a list in ShowResult
  (src/network-ui.js) so that rows for the single parts can follow: every L
  and C with its value or range, and the knob dependent ones with the value
  at both stops. Wanted by the user for a later stage. doc/advanced-mode.md,
  What is shown.

- part-editing:
  The part panel shows a selected part but only offers Solve. Wanted next to
  it: edit the value or range in place (rewriting the field text through
  SetValue), delete a part, wrap the selection in a series or parallel group,
  and mark the selected part's token in the text field. Marking inside an
  input is not possible with styling, so it needs selection ranges
  (setSelectionRange) or a mirror element; Scan already knows every token
  position. doc/advanced-mode.md, Selection.

- equation-radical-clearance:
  In the frequency equation with a tall denominator (several nested
  fractions under the square root) the bar of the radical comes very close
  to the bar of the fraction, so the two lines nearly merge. This is TeX's
  standard clearance (three rule thicknesses) but looks cramped in the
  Advanced mode at a font size of 26. Add some space above the radical in
  Sqrt or the denominator shift in Frac (src/equations.js), then check the
  basic images of tools/equations.js still match. doc/typesetting.md, TeX
  metrics.

- exit-shortcut:
  Early on an exit button for the app was discussed; the proposal was no
  button in the window but a keyboard shortcut (Ctrl+Q or Ctrl+W) in
  Electron and Tauri, since closing the window already quits and the web
  version has nothing to quit. The user did not decide. Ask again or drop it.

- nginx-cache-control:
  The nginx location that serves /thomson-calculator/ sends no Cache-Control,
  so browsers keep old modules and stylesheets and the page breaks after
  every update until a hard reload (doc/pitfalls.md). Add
  add_header Cache-Control "no-cache"; to the location in
  /etc/nginx/sites-available/ssl (the old location block there is commented
  out, the root /var/www/html/thomson-calculator is a symlink to src/).
  That file is outside the repository, so it is the user's decision. An
  alternative inside the repository is a version query on every module
  import, which is a lot of bookkeeping.

- dev-server-runtime-image:
  Under bun run web the equation image of the basic mode breaks after the
  solved field is changed: app.js sets img.src = svg/<name>-equation.svg at
  run time and the dev server only serves assets referenced from the HTML,
  so it returns the HTML page. It worked in the other runtimes. Reference all
  three images in the HTML (preloaded or as hidden img elements) and switch
  between them. Also: a lazily loaded module can stay stale in the dev
  server until it is restarted (doc/pitfalls.md).

- desktop-app-verification:
  The new icons (src-tauri/icons including icon.icns and the Windows
  Square logos, electron/icon.png) and the Advanced mode were only looked at
  in a browser. Run bun run start and bun run tauri:dev, check the window
  icon, the Advanced mode (dynamic import over file://, the saved config in
  the app's own profile), and build one package of each. Only Linux has been
  built so far.

- browser-tests:
  The DOM code of the Advanced mode (ShowNetworks, ShowResult, Select, the
  event wiring, restoring the saved state) is only checked by hand with the
  checklist in doc/advanced-mode.md. Two bugs that were found on the hosted
  page, the start order and the config clobbering, would have been caught by
  a test that loads app.html in a real DOM and reloads it. Candidates:
  happy-dom under bun test, or a small script that drives a headless
  Chrome. test/equations.test.js also holds the Advanced mode helpers and
  should be split into network-ui.test.js and equations.test.js when it
  grows further.

- trim-glyph-set:
  src/glyphs.js is 91 KB and is loaded with every page because app.js imports
  equations.js statically, although the basic mode needs none of it. Either
  import equations.js lazily with network-ui.js or let tools/glyphs.js emit
  only the glyphs the typesetter can ask for (the Greek letters and the
  uppercase set are the bulk). Measure what the basic mode really loads
  before and after.

- readme-update:
  README.md still says adjustable parts are not modelled and does not mention
  the Advanced mode, ranges in the part syntax, the trimmer symbol, doc/ or
  tools/. Update it together with the next feature, and keep it short: it is
  the user summary, doc/ is the specification.

- lc-meter-measurements:
  Identify the gang capacitors in the drawer (photos show four trimmer
  screws and section labels C1, C3, C4) by measuring each section at both
  stops and every 10 percent of the rotation, with the trimmers fully out
  and fully in, using the LC meter in ../lc-meter. The tables become user
  tapers (measured-tapers) and the test data for knob-curve; the procedure
  is at the end of doc/polyvaricon.md. Note whether capacitance really
  falls clockwise and which Mitsumi curve each section follows.

- opposed-ranges:
  A mechanism where one part grows while another shrinks with the same knob,
  for example an inductor on the shaft of a capacitor. Probably a reversed
  taper modifier (g becomes 1 - g) on a range written as usual. Decide when a
  real case turns up. doc/knob-curve.md, Open questions.

- cbm-443-verify:
  The CBM-443 data sheet we found is an anonymous generated PDF, not from
  the manufacturer; the pin table in it is unverified (doc/polyvaricon.md).
  Get the Hong Da data (hdic.com.hk) or measure the pins before using the
  pin assignment anywhere.
